  "notes": "West leads the ♠J which South wins. It looks like 12 tricks should be easy if clubs break no worse than 4-2. South will have 3 spade tricks, 4 heart tricks, the ♦A, and at least 4 club tricks.\n\nHowever, there is a danger. Suppose South cashes the ♥K and ♥Q, plays a club to the ♣A, and cashes dummy's hearts discarding diamonds. If a defender with 4 clubs also has 5 hearts he will be able to defeat the contract. When he gains the lead in clubs he will have a heart trick to cash.\n\nA safety play is available to guard against this distribution. After unblocking South's hearts, the first round of clubs should be ducked. South will have established a long club trick at a time when the defense cannot cash a heart trick. The ♣A is retained as an entry to dummy's hearts.",
  "commentary_paragraphs": [
    "West leads the ♠J which South wins. It looks like 12 tricks should be easy if clubs break no worse than 4-2. South will have 3 spade tricks, 4 heart tricks, the ♦A, and at least 4 club tricks.",
